From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:49:43 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/13] client: draw far regions from simplified meshes

At range 320 on a 512 MB handheld (Mali-400), a view across a lake drew
1.52 M vertices in ~1,300 draw calls at 3-4 FPS, and the driver's
per-draw vertex output buffers filled the RAM. 81 % of those vertices
were beyond 192 nodes, where a node is about one pixel at 320x240.

client_lod_distance (default 0, off): beyond it, regions of 2x2x2
mapblocks are drawn from one simplified mesh, made by the mesh threads
after the detail meshes (meshgen/lod.cpp). Each 2x2x2 nodes become one
cell: solid when at least 2 of the 8 are opaque cubes (the most common
one gives the texture, the upper layer the top), else liquid when one
is a liquid, else air. 2 nodes per cell is about 2 pixels at 208 nodes.

Faces between solid cells are left out. Faces on the region's sides
toward solid cells of the neighbour go to six skirt meshes, drawn only
while that neighbour shows detail, so the seam between detail and
simplified meshes stays closed. Light comes from the nodes across each
face.

The BFS culler decides per region, from the distance of its center: a
region is drawn simplified when any of its blocks passes the frustum and
occlusion tests and its mesh is ready (detail meshes until then). A
block change marks the regions that read it, remeshed at most every 2 s
while drawn. Unused simplified meshes expire with
client_unload_unused_data_timeout. Needs client_mesh_chunk 1 or 2.

Measured with an arm64 build on Mesa llvmpipe, same world and pose as
the handheld's lake test, client_lod_distance 208: 1,517,300 -> 679,198
vertices, 1,020 -> 504 draw calls, 13 -> 22 FPS; 4 % of the pixels
change, at the far shore's silhouettes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

